{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793407844,"nonce":"L9179i4089RlHR2Xk5IQ/j5ad7k=","message":"Build-time machine code landed in 2381ddbb3b320cc995e14d2c7ff05de38fabe13a (phases 1 to 4 at build time; the CI-served LLVM variant, phase 5, remains).\n\nWhat the commit does:\n- Both frontends' build scripts compile the library module with Cranelift for TARGET, two variants (OSR probes on for a ladder with LLVM, off for one that ends at Cranelift), against the static plugins' signatures (zrtl_io, zrtl_string, zrtl_math). A target this build's Cranelift lacks (a cross build) gets a cargo warning and no code.\n- The snapshot carries each variant as an image read in place: target and imports, sorted id indexes of functions and callees, then each function encoded on its own. Nothing decodes at load; placement decodes the matching variant's index and only the functions it places.\n- Placement declares each runtime import with the signature the code was compiled against (recorded per import), and declines if this process declared it differently.\n- tests/portable_targets.rs in both frontends compiles the library for every release target this build's Cranelift can compile for.\n\nFindings on the way:\n- Placement first declared imports with an empty signature; the warm-up thread's later JIT declaration of zyntax_alloc panicked with IncompatibleSignature, and the dead thread's first-compile mark hung class/loop_carried_object.py forever. Fixed by recording import signatures; the hang-on-panic itself is 5e2561a.\n- A plugin crate builds as cdylib+staticlib+rlib, so its rlib has no hash in its file name. With the plugins as build-dependencies, stable cargo (not nightly, whose build-dir layout separates units) built a build-script copy (build-override profile, opt-level 0) and the runtime copy into the same target/release/deps/libzrtl_io.rlib; zyntax_python then failed with two zrtl types. Aligning build-override with release fixed io and string but not math (getrandom resolves with different features in the two graphs). Fix: crates/zyntax_plugin_tables compiles the three plugins' sources as modules of an rlib for the build scripts.\n- The first format postcard-decoded both whole variants (1282 Lua functions, about 1 MB each) at import: [LOWER] imports went from 0.63 to 5.98 ms and zylua's empty script from 10.2 to 17.4 ms on the NUC. The read-in-place image removed it: compile 2.99 ms placed vs 2.91 ms without.\n\nNUC (x86_64, release, LLVM ladder, taskset 0-3, interleaved, wall ms median (range), placed vs ZYNTAX_DISABLE_PORTABLE_CODE=1):\n- zylua empty: 9.0 (8.5-14.4) vs 9.1 (8.5-9.8); default ladder 9.6 vs 9.7\n- mandelbrot 46.9 vs 66.3; method_call 83.7 vs 99.5; nbody 169.3 vs 191.4; op_overload 84.4 vs 95.9; records 96.3 vs 120.8; tokenize 165.8 vs 201.6; binary_trees 183.1 vs 195.1; pipeline 406.5 vs 427.2; the short call benches level\n- closure_call looked worse at 11 reps (el 10.3 vs 7.2) and level at 25 (el 6.7 vs 7.1)\n- zypy empty 13.2 vs 12.8; deltablue -n 1 153.0 vs 161.0; deltablue -n 50 161.4 vs 174.5 (PyPy 247.6); spectral-norm -n 1 61.6 vs 67.2; fannkuch -n 1 83.0 vs 90.0\n- raytrace-simple -n 1 407 vs 398 and go -n 1 407 vs 388 at 13 reps, ranges overlapping (380 to 1600 both arms); recheck on a quiet run\n\nConformance with placement on by default: Lua 46/46, Python 50/50 (Mac, debug); TS pinned corpus no regressions.\n\nRemaining: phase 5 (CI release workflow serving LLVM-optimised variants per target, fetched by build.rs when the build id matches); Windows placement is exercised only by CI's native build and the cross-target test's x86_64-pc-windows-msvc compile.","files":null}]}